          "score": 0.69096714,
          "snippet": "David Hume's skepticism regarding causality disrupted Immanuel Kant’s dogmatic slumber, prompting him to develop a revolutionary theory of experience grounded in a priori concepts. Although Kant initially explored solutions influenced by Hume in his pre-critical works, he ultimately rejected the idea that causal relations are derived from experience. Instead, Kant formulated synthetic a priori judgments as the foundation for metaphysics, positioning Hume as the philosopher who most closely approached but failed to solve this general problem."
        },
        {
